WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.344
A private security operations dashboard should support decisions, not merely display data.

00:00:06.344 --> 00:00:14.097
Start with the questions authorized users must answer and the actions they need to take.

00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:22.321
Then select views, measures, and workflows that make significant events and monitoring failures easy to recognize.

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:32.424
Combine useful inputs such as CCTV events, local vision alerts, endpoints, network activity, authentication, vulnerabilities, assets, sensors, and incident notes.

00:00:32.424 --> 00:00:37.828
Normalize shared fields while preserving original evidence and source context.

00:00:37.828 --> 00:00:42.762
Distinguish a quiet environment from a broken collection path.

00:00:42.762 --> 00:00:48.166
Effective triage considers impact, confidence, evidence, asset importance, and urgency.

00:00:48.166 --> 00:00:55.449
Group related activity, document human decisions, and feed false-positive outcomes back into careful tuning.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:01:00.853
Do not let uncertain automation make high-impact judgments about people.

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:07.667
Privacy, role-based access, data minimization, retention, and audit logging belong in the design.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:14.481
Reliability requires health monitoring, capacity management, tested backups, fallback procedures, and controlled updates.

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:21.294
The dashboard itself needs authentication, patching, segmentation, protected secrets, and secure remote access.

00:01:21.294 --> 00:01:27.168
The end state is practical situational awareness under accountable human control.

00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:40.091
A well-designed private dashboard helps people review evidence and respond responsibly while preserving resilience, limiting unnecessary collection, and protecting the sensitive information entrusted to the system.
